Here’s a **clean and complete implementation of Bubble Sort in C++**, with explanations:

---

### 🔁 Bubble Sort (C++ Code)

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    // Outer loop for each pass
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        bool swapped = false;

        // Inner loop for pairwise comparison
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            // Swap if current element is greater than next
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                swap(arr[j], arr[j + 1]);
                swapped = true;
            }
        }

        // Optimization: stop if no swaps occurred (already sorted)
        if (!swapped)
            break;
    }
}

// Function to print array
void printArray(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    cout << endl;
}

// Main function
int main() {
    int arr[] = {64, 25, 12, 22, 11};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    cout << "Original array: ";
    printArray(arr, n);

    bubbleSort(arr, n);

    cout << "Sorted array: ";
    printArray(arr, n);

    return 0;
}
```

---

### 🔍 How Bubble Sort Works:
- Compares adjacent pairs (`arr[j]` and `arr[j+1]`)
- Swaps them if out of order
- After each pass, the largest element "bubbles" to the end
- Repeats until array is sorted

---

### 📈 Time Complexity:
- Worst & Average: **O(n²)**
- Best case (already sorted with optimization): **O(n)**

